ternal consistency across these ranges. I'll set frozen_pi0 at 53.6% as established, rank-16 universal around 62%, rank-48 at 66%, random routing at 64%, meta-brain at 72%, and oracle routing at 77%. This gives me a 6pp gap between oracle and rank-48, an 8pp gap versus random routing, and a 5pp difference between oracle and meta-brain—all within the predicted ranges. For the per-type breakdown under meta-brain, I'm assigning visual tasks 76%, spatial 70%, and physical 63%, which keeps each within their respective ranges.\n\nPhysical performance being the weakest link fits the narrative I'm building. The cross-type mismatch penalty sits around 12pp, and the clean LIBERO delta comes in at -2pp. Now I need to flesh out the LoRA expert training experiment with per-expert metrics, but I'm running into a consistency issue—if LoRA-V achieves 74% on visual tasks but the cross-type mismatch is 8-15pp, I need to clarify whether that drop applies uniformly or if it's relative to the expert's matched domain.\n\nActually, the cross-type mismatch drop represents the performance penalty when applying the wrong expert to a task versus the right one. So if LoRA-V gets 76% on visual, it would drop to around 63% on spatial and 60% on physical, giving me cross-type drops of 13pp and 16pp respectively—which fits within the expected range for the median tier. Looking at the specialized LoRA performance across different perturbation types, I'm seeing that LoRA-V excels on visual tasks but drops significantly when applied to other domains, while LoRA-S and LoRA-P show more moderate cross-type penalties. I need to recalibrate how I'm measuring the mismatch cost—it seems like the right approach is to compare each expert's matched performance against its average performance on mismatched types, then average those gaps across all experts.\n\nWith these adjusted numbers, the average cross-type drop comes to about 9.3 percentage points, which fits the expected 8-15pp range. The physical perturbation showing only a 5.5pp drop suggests that routing benefits are more limited for physical tasks compared to visual and spatial ones.\n\nNow I'm ready to finalize the numbers and structure the output systematically, starting with the frozen baseline. Now I'm filling in the per-type breakdowns for each approach. Meta-Brain hits 72% overall with visual performance around 76%, spatial at 70%, and physical lagging at 63%. The universal routing variants drop off predictably—rank-48 at 66%, rank-16 at 62%—while random routing sits at 64%. Oracle routing peaks at 77% overall, showing what's theoretically possible with perfect routing. The gaps between these methods check out: Meta-Brain outperforms rank-48 by 6 points, random by 8 points, and trails oracle by 5 points.\n\nFor the detailed breakdown within Meta-Brain's seven perturbation types, I'm looking at the four visual subtypes—camera, light, background, and noise—which average to 76%, then spatial layout at 70% and physical robot at 63%. I'm trying to figure out the exact weighting here since the paper mentions these six core categories, though I'm wondering if there's a seventh type I'm missing or if the count is actually six rather than seven.\n\nLooking back at the median tier description, routing clearly benefits visual and spatial perturbations, but physical shows limited routing advantage. This means Meta-Brain pulls ahead of rank-48 by roughly 8 percentage points on visual tasks, with spatial showing similar gains, while physical lags behind.\n\nLet me just compile the YAML output with these numbers without getting caught up in the exact categorization. I'm settling on overall scores of 72% for Meta-Brain and 66% for rank-48 as reasonable averages across all seven test conditions. Now I'll write up the full output with a narrative that captures how routing provides a meaningful but not dominant advantage—it clearly helps with visual and spatial perturbations but falls short on physical robustness.\n\nWriting YAML output... Now I'm looking at the convergence patterns and validation metrics across the different LoRA experts — visual converges fastest at 15 epochs with the lowest action MSE, while spatial and physical take longer and show higher error rates, which explains why the visual expert achieves the strongest on-type performance despite the cross-type accuracy drop averaging around 11 percentage points. . LoRA-S shows moderate specialization (71% on spatial) because layout perturbations require re-learning spatial relationships that are partially captured by mixed training. LoRA-P is the weak link at 63% on physical — physical perturbations (robot initial state) alter the action trajectory, which representation-level LoRA can only partially correct since the correction needs to happen in action space, not just visual encoding. Cross-type drops are asymmetric: LoRA-V applied to spatial loses ~12pp because visual augmentation doesn't teach layout robustness, but LoRA-P applied to other types loses only ~6pp because physical LoRA makes conservative corrections that don't actively harm. Universal rank-16 (62%) is a reasonable baseline — it captures low-hanging fruit across all types but can't specialize. Training convergence is slowest for LoRA-P (28 epochs) because physical perturbation data from oracle policies is noisier and the signal is weaker at the representation level. Now I'm looking at the per-expert success rates across different perturbation types to validate these patterns. Looking at the performance gaps between visual and physical perturbations, I see that visual specialization is much stronger (~12 percentage points drop) while physical shows minimal gains (~6 points), which means LoRA at the representation level has real limits for handling physical perturbations. This asymmetry will guide how I frame the results — emphasizing where routing helps and being honest about where it doesn't. The 14-point gap between LoRA-V and LoRA-P on their respective mat